using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

namespace OpenScct.Master.Web;

/// <summary>Authored player documentation. HTML here is trusted source content, never request data.</summary>
internal static class WikiContent
{
    internal sealed record Category(string Id, string Title, string Description);
    internal sealed record Section(string Id, string Title, string Html);
    internal sealed record Article(string Slug, string Category, string Title, string Summary, string Keywords,
        Section[] Sections, string[] Related);

    public static IReadOnlyList<Category> Categories { get; } =
    [
        new("start", "Getting started", "Installation, session access and settings."),
        new("gameplay", "Gameplay", "Roles, equipment, movement and objectives."),
        new("community", "Online play", "Hosting, hubs, chat, recordings and player stats."),
        new("editor", "Map editor", "Map projects, geometry, events, lighting and testing."),
    ];

    // Maintenance sources: SVM-Rewrite/docs/{LAUNCHER,LINUX,MENUS,MODES,GADGETS,ONLINE,VOICE,DEMOS,
    // PLAYER-STATS,PLAYER-HUB,SCOREBOARD}.md; Equipment/SpyCloak.cs; Movement/README.md.
    // Editor claims: OpenSCCT-Editor/README.md, docs/FORMAT.md, app/Scripts/Shell/BuildActions.cs,
    // Gameplay/AnalysisPart.cs and the actor/mission/identity validation and compiler sources.
    // These guides deliberately describe supported workflows without implying an editor public release.
    public static IReadOnlyList<Article> Articles { get; } =
    [
        new("getting-started", "start", "Installation and first session", "Installing the launcher and joining an online lobby.", "install launcher windows download setup join discord account",
        [
            new("install", "Install and launch", "<ol><li>Open the <a href=\"/download\">download page</a> and choose the launcher for your platform.</li><li>Keep the launcher in a writable folder. Start it and choose <strong>Install</strong>; it downloads the game and required content.</li><li>When the launcher says <strong>Ready to play</strong>, choose <strong>PLAY</strong>.</li></ol><p>You do not need to select an original Chaos Theory installation. The launcher checks for game and content updates. Its <strong>Maps</strong> window adds optional maps later.</p>"),
            new("learn", "Tutorials and role controls", "<p>Open <strong>Tutorials</strong> in the game. Spy and Mercenary Tutorial Maps teach each role; <strong>Visit a Map</strong> lets you explore alone. Check your role's bindings in <strong>Options → Spy</strong> or <strong>Options → Mercenary</strong>.</p><p>Spies use a third-person view and mobility to complete objectives. Mercenaries defend in first person. The roles have different equipment, views and objectives.</p>"),
            new("join", "Join a match", "<ol><li>Choose <strong>Find a Session</strong>, or open the website's <a href=\"/servers\">Servers</a> page.</li><li>Join a compatible session. Enter its password or sign in if required.</li><li>Choose a side, finish any missing-map download and mark yourself ready.</li></ol><p>Discord sign-in connects your player account to your progress. Website sign-in and game sign-in are separate; signing in here does not sign in the running game. Guest access depends on the server.</p>"),
        ], ["spy", "mercenary", "practice", "linux"]),
        new("linux", "start", "Linux and Steam Deck", "Native launcher and Flatpak installation, updates and requirements.", "linux steam deck flatpak proton wine x86-64 install",
        [
            new("launcher", "Native launcher", "<p>The native x86-64 launcher installs and updates the Linux game without Wine, Proton or a system .NET installation. Download the Linux launcher archive from <a href=\"/download\">Download</a>, extract it and run <code>./install.sh</code> from its extracted folder. This installs a user desktop entry and registers website Join links without sudo.</p><p>The portable binary also works from a writable folder: give it execute permission with <code>chmod +x OpenSCCT.Launcher-linux-x64</code>, then run it. A desktop with X11 or XWayland, working graphics drivers and the usual desktop audio libraries is required. ARM and musl-based distributions are outside this package's support.</p>"),
            new("dependencies", "Native dependencies", "<p>The launcher needs the distribution libraries providing <code>libX11</code>, <code>libICE</code>, <code>libSM</code>, <code>fontconfig</code>, OpenSSL 3, the C/C++ runtimes, zlib and CA certificates. Wayland desktops also need XWayland. These usually come with a full desktop; minimal installs may need extra packages. The game also needs working Vulkan/OpenGL drivers and audio libraries.</p><p>On Fedora 45, install <code>openssl3-libs</code>; the default OpenSSL 4 package does not provide the OpenSSL 3 libraries used by the bundled .NET runtime. The launcher bundles .NET itself, so installing a separate system SDK is not a normal player requirement.</p>"),
            new("flatpak", "Flatpak alternative", "<p>The Flatpak bundle starts the game directly. Install the downloaded bundle with <code>flatpak install --user ./OpenSCCT-linux-x86_64.flatpak</code>, then launch <code>flatpak run io.github.Lumbridge.OpenSCCT</code>. Flathub must be configured for its runtime dependencies.</p><p>Choose <strong>Download and play</strong> on first run. Content downloads are separate from the application bundle. Update the application by installing a newer bundle; the native launcher's automatic game updater does not update Flatpak installations.</p>"),
            new("deck", "Steam Deck", "<p>In Desktop Mode, add the installed application as a non-Steam game, then open it from Gaming Mode. Use a Gamepad Steam Input layout and leave compatibility-tool overrides off. Configure the two roles separately in Options.</p><p>Linux gameplay and Steam Deck support are experimental. Steam Deck hardware validation is still pending; controller mapping, microphone capture and Gaming Mode behavior need device testing. The native launcher manages game updates; Flatpak application updates require installing a newer bundle.</p>"),
        ], ["getting-started", "controls-and-hud", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("spy", "gameplay", "Playing as a spy", "Spy movement, equipment and objective interactions.", "spy third person shocker stealth cloak attack",
        [
            new("role", "Objectives and routes", "<p>Read the map's objective text and your HUD before choosing a route. In Story, your team attacks objectives while mercenaries defend. Disk Hunt changes the job to collecting disks. A useful distraction creates an opening for your teammate; chasing a defender is not always the fastest way to win.</p><p>The third-person view can show nearby corners and climbs. Explore alternate paths during a map visit, then learn where those paths meet the objective.</p>"),
            new("equipment", "Shocker and gadgets", "<p>The Shocker stuns targets and disables supported devices; it does not deal health damage. It uses the spy's energy. Drawing it changes how your attack inputs behave: with the weapon holstered, quick-use can throw a grenade instead of firing.</p><p>Smoke, chaff and sticky cameras serve different jobs. Smoke can cover an approach, chaff disrupts electronics, and sticky cameras provide a remote view and gas. Pick a loadout for the route and coordinate limited charges with your teammate.</p>"),
            new("survive", "Escape and reset", "<p>Plan an exit before starting an interaction. Break line of sight, use a different level or route, and avoid repeating the same exposed approach. Cloak is a separate built-in ability with its own binding and energy cost; drawing your weapon and other conditions can prevent or interrupt it.</p><p>Use Tutorials to practice movement and contextual interactions without a match timer. Check your configured bindings when a prompt names an action.</p>"),
        ], ["movement", "gadgets-and-loadouts", "modes-and-objectives", "practice"]),
        new("mercenary", "gameplay", "Playing as a mercenary", "Mercenary weapons, detection equipment and objective defense.", "merc mercenary rifle flashlight torch laser scope emf motion",
        [
            new("defend", "Objective defense", "<p>Mercenaries play in first person. Watch how routes connect to objectives and communicate which route you can cover. An alarm or a glimpse of a spy is information to assess, not a reason for both defenders to abandon the same objective.</p><p>Check the selected mode and lives before the round. Deathmatch and Merc Deathmatch ask for different priorities from a Story defense.</p>"),
            new("kit", "Weapons and detection equipment", "<p>The rifle has reload and fire-mode actions; the scope helps with distant targets. The torch illuminates a route, while the laser can mark a spy kept in its beam. Vision modes provide different information: motion tracking is useful against movement and EMF highlights electronic equipment.</p><p>Mines and presence detectors cover chosen approaches, the camera network offers remote views where the map provides cameras, and the gas mask protects against smoke and gas while its air lasts. Host restrictions and your loadout determine what you can equip.</p>"),
            new("habits", "Patrol and aim settings", "<p>Reload in cover and keep an escape route when checking a tight corner. Check floors, ledges and overhead routes as well as eye level. A device complements your patrol; it does not replace watching the objective.</p><p>Use <strong>Options → Mercenary</strong> to tune your aim, controller bindings and crosshair. Test changes in a quiet session so you can tell how scope and normal aim feel separately.</p>"),
        ], ["gadgets-and-loadouts", "modes-and-objectives", "controls-and-hud", "voice"]),
        new("modes-and-objectives", "gameplay", "Modes and objectives", "Win conditions and objective interactions for each mode.", "story disk hunt deathmatch extraction sabotage neutralization mission bomb terminal",
        [
            new("modes", "Win conditions", "<dl><dt><strong>Story</strong></dt><dd>Spies complete the map's objectives; mercenaries defend. Objectives may involve terminals, a disk and drop zone, or bomb targets.</dd><dt><strong>Disk Hunt</strong></dt><dd>Spies collect the required disks. Mercenaries stop them and return dropped disks.</dd><dt><strong>Deathmatch</strong></dt><dd>Exhaust the opposing team's lives before time runs out.</dd><dt><strong>Merc Deathmatch</strong></dt><dd>Free-for-all mercenary combat with a kill limit.</dd></dl><p>Extraction, Sabotage and Neutralization are additional modes for custom maps authored to support them. A map's variant name alone does not tell you its actual game mode.</p>"),
            new("interact", "Use the objective prompt", "<p>Move close enough to the target and use its contextual interaction. Follow the available action and HUD progress. A Story disk needs its matching drop zone; reaching a terminal is not the same task as carrying a disk or planting a charge.</p><p>In Disk Hunt, death can drop carried disks back into play. Mercenaries can return dropped disks by walking over them. Defend the path to the target as well as the target itself.</p>"),
            new("rules", "Check the lobby rules", "<p>Time limits, lives, respawn delays and objective counts start from the map's settings and can be changed by the host. Look at the lobby before readying up. A new map or mode can reload those defaults.</p><p>The HUD and results describe the round actually being played. Use those values when learning a map rather than assuming every variant has the same objective count.</p>"),
        ], ["spy", "mercenary", "maps", "editor-objectives"]),
        new("movement", "gameplay", "Movement and traversal", "Ladders, ledges, pipes and role-specific movement.", "walk run crouch roll jump climb ladder pipe ledge wall coop",
        [
            new("basics", "Basic controls", "<p>Practice walking, changing speed, crouching and jumping before combining them with combat. Your input settings can use Hold or Toggle behavior for supported actions, so check what you have configured in your role's Options page.</p><p>Spies have a wider traversal set, including rolling and routes through ledges, pipes and other authored traversal features. Both roles can use ladders. A visible prop is not automatically a climbable feature; the map must support the interaction.</p>"),
            new("routes", "Learn a route in parts", "<ol><li>Use <strong>Tutorials → Visit a Map</strong> to explore a route alone.</li><li>Approach a climb from more than one angle and watch for the contextual action.</li><li>Practice mounting, moving along and leaving the feature.</li><li>Connect it to an objective approach and a retreat.</li></ol><p>Test your movement with the weapon holstered as well as drawn. Weapon and movement states can change which actions are available.</p>"),
            new("trouble", "When a climb does not engage", "<p>Move away and line up again instead of holding several conflicting actions. Check your bindings and try the tutorial version of the move. A map-specific failure may be a missing or misplaced traversal feature.</p><p>For a repeatable problem, record the map, side, direction and exact approach. The in-game Help screen can include position and movement details in a bug report.</p>"),
        ], ["practice", "controls-and-hud", "editor-blockout", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("gadgets-and-loadouts", "gameplay", "Gadgets, loadouts and cloak", "Equipment selection, gadget charges, refills and cloak energy.", "gadget gear loadout mine cam smoke chaff cloak energy backpack",
        [
            new("loadout", "Choose a loadout", "<p>Use <strong>Change Gear</strong> to choose from the equipment permitted for your side and the host's rules. Select a gadget slot before using it. Grenades use a hold-and-release throw, while other gadgets follow their own contextual use.</p><p>Spies commonly use smoke, chaff, sticky cameras and a heartbeat sensor. Mercenaries can equip grenades, mines, camera-network access, a gas mask and other supported equipment. Learn one tool at a time in a practice session.</p>"),
            new("charges", "Charges and refills", "<p>Gadget charges are limited. An ammo crate replenishes rifle ammunition and a supported Sabotage bomb, rather than refilling every gadget. A teammate's backpack can share gadget charges. Recovering a sticky camera or removing a mine through its interaction can return the appropriate charge.</p><p>Device placement needs a valid surface and enough space. If a gadget refuses to fire or place, move away from the nearby wall, confirm the selected slot and check the remaining charges.</p>"),
            new("cloak", "Built-in spy cloak", "<p>Cloak has its own action under Spy bindings and does not occupy a gadget slot. Choose Hold or Toggle behavior in Options. It consumes energy while active and recharges after the suit is no longer engaged.</p><p>Activation requires positive energy, a holstered weapon and a permitted movement state. Moving too fast, sticky shock and rain are among the conditions that can prevent use. Watch your energy gauge; a little energy allows activation but does not promise a long escape.</p>"),
        ], ["spy", "mercenary", "practice", "controls-and-hud"]),
        new("practice", "gameplay", "Tutorials and practice", "Tutorial maps, map visits, bots and practice-server rules.", "tutorial practice bots training visit map solo heal regen",
        [
            new("tutorials", "Follow the tutorials", "<p>Open <strong>Tutorials</strong> from the main menu. It includes View HUD, Tips, Gadget Details, Spy Tutorial Maps, Mercenary Tutorial Maps and Visit a Map. Tips name actions using your current bindings.</p><p>Tutorials and map visits run offline and alone, without a time limit and with unlimited lives. Completed tutorials are stored locally. Visit a Map shows the map's help paths so you can learn an approach without a live match.</p>"),
            new("session", "Create a practice session", "<p>Choose <strong>Create a Session</strong>, select a map and use the bot controls to fill slots or set exact teams. Enable <strong>Practice</strong> in the rules when you want unlimited lives, quick respawns and the practice commands.</p><p>Practice chat commands act on you: <code>!heal</code>, <code>!regen</code>, <code>!tank</code>, <code>!kill</code> and <code>!ammo</code>. Enter <code>!practice</code> for the server's help. These commands are available only on practice servers.</p>"),
            new("progress", "Practice and progress", "<p>A round that used Practice does not award career stats or XP. Local match history can still record a practice scoreboard, which is labelled accordingly. Bots are useful for trying controls and routes, but are not a substitute for testing a full human match.</p><p>Keep one goal per session: a movement route, one gadget, a rifle setting or a particular objective approach. Repeat it on both sides to understand what the opponent can see.</p>"),
        ], ["movement", "gadgets-and-loadouts", "hosting", "progression-and-stats"]),
        new("hosting", "community", "Join and host a session", "Joining sessions, configuring lobbies and network access.", "host server listen public lan network ip port password ready",
        [
            new("join", "Joining", "<p>Use <strong>Find a Session</strong> for listed games, <strong>Join with IP</strong> for a known address, or <a href=\"/servers\">Servers</a> on the website. Website Join links open the installed launcher/game. Run the Windows launcher once, or the Linux archive's installer, to register the link handler.</p><p>Finish required map downloads and choose a side before readying up. A protocol or content mismatch needs an update; retrying the same old build will not resolve it.</p>"),
            new("host", "Hosting from the game", "<ol><li>Open <strong>Create a Session</strong> and choose a map and supported mode.</li><li>Review Game, Rules and Hosting, including player slots, bots, password, practice and discovery.</li><li>Create the lobby, wait for players and check the final rules before starting.</li></ol><p>The host controls session setup. Changing map or rules can reset readiness so everyone can review the change. LAN and self-hosted play can run without the public master service.</p>"),
            new("network", "Connection checks", "<p>A public listing and an actual reachable game connection are separate steps. Allow the game through your firewall. The default game socket uses UDP 28888; LAN discovery uses UDP 28889. A custom game port needs matching firewall/router settings.</p><p>Connection assistance can try direct, NAT-assisted and relay paths when available. If joining fails, keep the specific refusal or timeout text and note whether LAN, direct IP or a public listing was used. Test from another network when checking public reachability.</p>"),
        ], ["getting-started", "maps", "voice", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("maps", "community", "Maps and content downloads", "Map variants, optional downloads and content compatibility.", "map maps content pack download enhanced stock community 3v3 variant",
        [
            new("browse", "Choose a map", "<p>The map browser can filter All installed maps, Enhanced, Stock, Community and 3v3 optimised maps. Search and the selected mode narrow the results further. Variants can change geometry, objectives and default rules, so check the selected entry rather than relying on the base name.</p><p>Story and Disk Hunt need authored objectives. Deathmatch modes have different requirements. Custom maps can supply additional supported objective modes.</p>"),
            new("install", "Download content", "<p>The launcher's <strong>Maps</strong> window downloads ready-to-play packs, manages optional maps and checks for updates. The game can also download a missing map when you join a lobby. Allow that download to finish before readying up.</p><p>Required shared packs contain common game content. A map pack alone does not replace those shared files. The normal launcher workflow installs them for you; converting original files is a separate developer workflow.</p>"),
            new("mismatch", "When a map is incompatible", "<p>Two copies of the same map code can contain different data. Update the game and its content, then reconnect. Check for custom content overrides if the launcher says everything is current but the game still loads another copy.</p><p>Do not rename a map folder to try to fix a content hash mismatch. Keep the reported map code and mismatch text for troubleshooting. A demo also requires compatible map content when played later.</p>"),
        ], ["hosting", "demos", "editor-import-export", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("social-hubs", "community", "Social hubs and match pads", "Hub access, proximity chat, match pads and activities.", "hub social community proximity voice pad laptop assault course aim range emote jukebox",
        [
            new("visit", "Visit a hub", "<p>Choose <strong>Hub</strong> from the game's main menu, pick a listed hub and join. You can also join by address or host your own. Hubs are shared social spaces for spies and mercenaries, separate from the website's player hub.</p><p>Proximity voice lets nearby players hear you. Use the player list for voice volume and mute controls, and the chat channel selector for everyone, team or nearby text chat. Follow the on-screen hub controls, which differ from a normal match.</p>"),
            new("pads", "Start a match from a pad", "<p>Walk onto a match pad and use its laptop to choose match settings. Ready up when your group is set. Players move to a match server for the round and return to the hub's pads afterward. Watching a pad's match is a separate action from joining its team.</p><p>A hub hosted inside someone's game closes when that host leaves. Use a dedicated hub when you want the social space to continue independently of one player's visit.</p>"),
            new("activities", "Activities between matches", "<p>The hub includes movement/traversal activities, an assault course, an aim range, emotes and a jukebox. Use the activity prompt to start and its stop action to return to ordinary hub movement. Local signs explain the area and its controls.</p><p>Activity scores belong to the hub's own activities, not the website's counted-round career leaderboards. Use the range to tune aim and the course to learn movement, then test those skills in a real map.</p>"),
        ], ["voice", "hosting", "practice", "progression-and-stats"]),
        new("voice", "community", "Voice and text chat", "Microphone settings, voice routing and text-chat channels.", "voice microphone push to talk mute volume echo activation audio text chat channel",
        [
            new("setup", "Set up your microphone", "<ol><li>Open <strong>Options → Voice</strong> and enable voice chat.</li><li>Choose the input device and use <strong>Test my microphone</strong>.</li><li>Choose push to talk or voice activation, then adjust input gain and the activation threshold.</li></ol><p>Use the displayed talk and team-talk bindings instead of assuming your current keys match another player's. Options also provides microphone mute, output volume, automatic gain and noise suppression.</p>"),
            new("channels", "Who can hear you", "<p>The session's voice mode and gameplay rules decide who receives a message. Team choice, life/spectator state, grabs and hacked communications can affect routing. Lobby and hub rules can differ from an active round.</p><p>Use the lobby or hub player list to mute a player or change their volume. A local mute is different from muting your own microphone. Speaking indicators help you tell whether transmission is being detected.</p>"),
            new("text", "Text chat", "<p>Use the configured chat or team-chat action to open the chat editor. Check the channel shown by the editor before sending. The available channels and who receives a message depend on whether you are in a lobby, match or social hub.</p><p>In hubs, the channel selector includes everyone, team and nearby chat. Chat and voice use separate controls; sending team text does not change the session's voice routing mode.</p>"),
            new("fix", "If voice sounds wrong", "<p>Test the selected microphone first, then check your operating system's input permissions and device volume. If voice activation never opens, lower the threshold while watching the input meter. If it stays open, increase the threshold or switch to push to talk.</p><p>A headset reduces speaker feedback. Echo cancellation is experimental and starts disabled. When reporting a problem, distinguish microphone capture, transmission and playback; mention the voice mode and whether it occurs in a lobby or round.</p>"),
        ], ["controls-and-hud", "hosting", "social-hubs", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("demos", "community", "Demos and match history", "Recording, playback, local copies and scoreboard history.", "demo replay record recording playback match history scoreboard",
        [
            new("record", "Record a match", "<p>Enable <strong>Automatically save demos</strong> in the lobby's Match demos panel, Create Session or Demos library. This is your preference; another player's recording choice does not enable yours. A compatible server records one full spectator stream for requesting players.</p><p>After the round, stay connected until <strong>Demo saved</strong> appears. Leaving during an unfinished download can cancel that copy. Find completed recordings under <strong>Demos → On this computer</strong>.</p>"),
            new("watch", "Playback and local copies", "<p>The library offers local recordings and Recent online recordings for signed-in players. <strong>Watch</strong> starts playback; <strong>Keep</strong> saves a permanent local copy instead of relying on the expiring cache.</p><p>The playback dock controls pause, speed, timeline, watched player and camera mode. First person, third person and free camera offer different views of the same recorded round. A demo stores game data rather than video, so it needs a compatible game protocol and matching map content.</p>"),
            new("history", "Scoreboard retention", "<p><strong>Demos → Match history</strong> includes completed scoreboards even when no replay exists. You can also use the lobby's <strong>Last game → View scoreboard</strong>. Detailed role counters explain what happened beyond kills and deaths.</p><p>Deleting or expiring a replay does not delete its scoreboard. An unavailable value is shown as an em dash. Categories can overlap: a headshot can also be a weapon kill, so adding every counter does not produce a meaningful total.</p>"),
        ], ["progression-and-stats", "maps", "practice"]),
        new("progression-and-stats", "community", "Progression, stats and privacy", "Counted rounds, career counters, XP and public visibility.", "stats statistic xp level prestige win rate kd kill death leaderboard account privacy achievement",
        [
            new("counted", "Which rounds count", "<p>Career stats, XP and achievements come from rounds accepted by the official-server progression rules. A playable session is not automatically a counted session. Practice rounds do not award progress; the website's match details label whether stats and XP were included.</p><p>Local history can still retain private, offline, guest and practice scoreboards. Those records are useful for review, but are separate from trusted career totals. Historical data that was never reported cannot be recreated by a new website view.</p>"),
            new("numbers", "Career counters and ratios", "<p>Open <strong>Stats</strong> in the game or browse a public <a href=\"/players\">player profile</a>. Career totals include role-specific combat, gadgets, objectives, weapons and base-map wins. Win rate needs completed rounds; K/D needs deaths. An em dash means unavailable data or a missing denominator.</p><p>Some counters overlap. Neutralisations can include both a knockout and a later kill. A headshot can also count toward a weapon total. Accuracy uses total hits divided by total shots, rather than an average of per-round percentages.</p>"),
            new("account", "XP, prestige and privacy", "<p>Counted rounds and achievements award XP toward levels and unlocks. Prestige resets the current level and its level-specific unlocks; read the account confirmation before submitting it.</p><p><a href=\"/leaderboards\">Leaderboards</a> use lifetime reported totals. Win-rate rankings need at least 10 counted rounds. Detailed counters may omit older rounds from before those counters were tracked. Rankings exclude private, unnamed and actively banned accounts. Manage public visibility under <strong>My account → Privacy</strong>. Making a profile private hides it from public search and rankings, and public match rows use a private-player label.</p>"),
        ], ["demos", "practice", "getting-started"]),
        new("controls-and-hud", "start", "Controls, aim and HUD", "Role bindings, aim settings, controllers and HUD layout.", "controls keybind binding controller gamepad hud crosshair sensitivity options settings scoreboard",
        [
            new("bindings", "Configure each role", "<p>Options has separate <strong>Spy</strong> and <strong>Mercenary</strong> pages for Bindings, Mouse &amp; Aim and Controller. Search by action or choose a category such as movement, combat, gadgets, vision or communication. Keyboard, mouse and controller inputs can be adjusted without assuming both roles use the same layout.</p><p>Choose an input slot to replace a binding, or add another binding without replacing the current one. Supported actions show Hold/Toggle behavior. Review shared-input notes when one button has more than one contextual job.</p>"),
            new("aim", "Aim and display settings", "<p>Start with normal aim, then test scope/camera sensitivity separately. Shared controller options handle stick behavior and vibration; role controller tuning lives under that role. Mercenary includes its custom crosshair editor and preview.</p><p>Display and Performance separate screen setup from frame caps, vertical sync and pacing settings. Apply a small number of changes at a time and test them in a quiet session.</p>"),
            new("hud", "HUD layout and scoreboard", "<p>The HUD page controls information visibility; <strong>HUD editor</strong> offers a drag preview, searchable elements and aligned properties. Its More menu includes presets, resets, copying and importing. Use Apply or Save &amp; Close to keep staged edits.</p><p>The scoreboard has separate toggle and hold actions; check your bindings for both. Its public scores update separately from delayed live combat counters. Full detailed totals arrive when the server ends the round, so a live counter is not an immediate confirmation of a hidden interaction.</p>"),
        ], ["voice", "movement", "gadgets-and-loadouts", "progression-and-stats"]),
        new("troubleshooting", "start", "Troubleshooting", "Checks for installation, connection, content and settings errors.", "help problem error crash download update connect join launch black screen bug",
        [
            new("install", "Installation or update stops", "<p>Keep the launcher in a writable folder and check free disk space and network access. Retry the displayed task; completed downloads are retained where supported. If a release check fails, an installed build can still offer <strong>Play current version</strong>.</p><p>Use the launcher's Settings folder shortcuts to inspect the relevant installation. Game settings live separately from downloaded builds. Avoid deleting your settings or maps as the first response to an update error.</p>"),
            new("joining", "A session will not join", "<p>Read the connection screen's actual error. A password refusal, account restriction, protocol mismatch, map mismatch and network timeout need different fixes. Update the launcher/game/content, confirm the password and check whether the server permits guests.</p><p>If a website Join button opens nothing, run the Windows launcher once, or install the Linux launcher archive's desktop integration. You can still join from the game's browser. Website sign-in does not authenticate the game.</p>"),
            new("report", "Bug report details", "<p>When a problem repeats, open <a href=\"/help/report-bug\">Report a bug</a>. Include what you did, what happened and what you expected, plus the version, platform, map, mode, side and hosting/joining status. In-game Help can prefill available match and position details.</p><p>Reduce the steps until another player could repeat them. Review your report before publishing. Screenshots can help show a visual failure; remove personal information and do not attach original game files or converted content packs.</p>"),
        ], ["getting-started", "linux", "hosting", "maps"]),
        new("contributing", "community", "Contributing and source builds", "Testing, documentation, build requirements and pull requests.", "contribute development build source dotnet godot github pull request docs test",
        [
            new("help", "Testing and documentation contributions", "<p>Contributions can include bug reports, verified setup instructions, documentation corrections and playtest results. Start with <a href=\"/help\">Help &amp; feedback</a> and existing issues so your work can build on what is already known.</p><p>For a documentation correction, name the page and the behavior you checked. For gameplay reports, distinguish an automated test from something observed during a real match. State any remaining manual checks plainly.</p>"),
            new("build", "Build the project", "<p>Clone the OpenSCCT source and use the .NET SDK pinned by its <code>global.json</code>. From the repository root, run <code>dotnet build OpenSCCT.slnx</code>. The Godot client is a separate project under <code>game/</code> and needs the Godot .NET version pinned in <code>godot-version.txt</code>.</p><p>Use <code>tools/Get-Godot.ps1</code> to obtain that engine version and build <code>game/OpenScctGame.csproj</code> before launching the client. Simulation tests can run without original assets; rendering/play needs compatible installed content. Consult the source tree's CONTRIBUTING and development reference before changing shared systems.</p>"),
            new("review", "Branches, checks and pull requests", "<p>Use a focused branch or fork, preserve unrelated work and describe the behavior your change affects. Run checks appropriate to that behavior, then open a pull request from the source repository. GitHub sign-in is needed for contributions there; reading this wiki needs no account.</p><p>Keep original game files, converted assets and decompiled output out of contributions. Explain which tests ran and which multiplayer or visual checks still need a person.</p>"),
        ], ["troubleshooting", "editor-getting-started", "progression-and-stats"]),
        new("editor-getting-started", "editor", "Editor: getting started", "Editor requirements, project formats and an initial test map.", "editor setup osmap svmap new build play install development",
        [
            new("status", "Editor access and requirements", "<p>OpenSCCT Editor is a separate tool in early development. These guides describe its current project workflow; the normal game launcher does not install the editor. If you have an editor checkout, start with its <code>Run-Editor.cmd</code>. It synchronizes the pinned OpenSCCT dependency, builds the app and opens it.</p><p>A source setup needs the pinned .NET SDK, Godot .NET and compatible OpenSCCT libraries/content. Installed game content can supply maps and class data. Use the versions pinned by the editor checkout rather than combining unrelated builds.</p>"),
            new("project", "Keep the editable project", "<p>Your source map is an <code>.osmap</code> project folder. It keeps brushes, actors, traversal features, settings and workspace state. Save and back up the whole project, including its local assets.</p><p>A compiled <strong>SVMap</strong> folder is what OpenSCCT plays. It contains derived collision, BSP and other runtime data. Keep the .osmap as your source: a compiled folder does not preserve every editing detail.</p>"),
            new("first", "Create and test a room", "<ol><li>Create a new map and save the project with a clear map ID.</li><li>Make a simple room and place safe Spy and Mercenary starts.</li><li>Add light and inspect the layout from both sides.</li><li>Choose <strong>Build → Check Map</strong> and resolve errors.</li><li>Choose <strong>Build → Build and Play</strong>, then test each side.</li></ol><p>The command palette lists actions and descriptions when you cannot find a panel or tool. Confirm the room builds and loads before adding other map systems.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-blockout", "editor-testing", "editor-import-export"]),
        new("editor-blockout", "editor", "Editor: blockout and traversal", "Brush geometry, traversal features and route validation.", "editor geometry brush csg blockout ledge pipe ge ladder grid collision",
        [
            new("layout", "Layout and brushes", "<p>Block out the map with simple brushes. CSG order matters: later operations can change earlier solid/open space. Keep the grid useful, name major areas and use layers or storeys to focus on one part of the layout.</p><p>Give both sides safe starts, an objective approach and at least one alternate route. Test distances in the game early; a room that looks spacious from the editor camera may play very differently from a mercenary's first-person view.</p>"),
            new("traversal", "Traversal features", "<p>Geometric events (GEs) describe supported traversal features such as ledges and pipes. Ledge detection can suggest features; inspect their direction, ends, joins and IDs rather than treating every suggestion as finished.</p><p>Use the GE tools to probe a ledge, fit it flush to its edge and join corners. Keep the surrounding collision clear for mounting and dismounting. Player-count activation rules can change which features exist in a particular match layout.</p>"),
            new("verify", "Verify the route", "<p>Run reachability analysis for each side, then walk the route yourself. Test entry, exit and a failed approach. Check a route with the map's own rules as well as an isolated camera-start test.</p><p>Do not rely on bot navigation alone to certify a spy route. A player traversal feature and a bot's reachable graph answer different questions. Keep geometry simple until movement, collision and sight lines work.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-getting-started", "editor-testing", "movement"]),
        new("editor-actors-and-events", "editor", "Editor: actors, devices and events", "Actor placement, Event/Tag links and device state changes.", "editor actor event tag wire trigger door mover camera network security magic",
        [
            new("place", "Place and inspect actors", "<p>Use Classes to find a supported actor type, place it, then inspect its properties. Names help you find objects; properties define their behavior. Use layers and the Outliner to keep devices, gameplay and decoration manageable.</p><p>Security tools help place and align cameras, beams and detectors to a surface. Inspect the previewed cone or range and test from the player's viewpoint. A device embedded in a wall may look correct in an overview but fail in play.</p>"),
            new("connect", "Wire a small event chain", "<p>Event/Tag links connect senders to receivers. Assign identifiers to each chain, link the intended receiver and inspect the Connections/event graph. Watch for dangling links, accidental shared tags and cycles that fire more than expected.</p><p>Use the specialized workbenches for movers, camera networks, SMagicEvent timelines and zone actions. Set a door's keys and timing in its mover controls; organize cameras into a valid network rather than only placing independent cameras.</p>"),
            new("test", "Test state changes", "<p>Build and play after one link is added. Activate it once, activate it again, and test any reset condition. Check what happens after respawn and at a new round. Look at the event graph when several devices react unexpectedly.</p><p>Keep a simple test version of a chain before expanding it. Map Check and deep checks find structural problems, while actual play confirms the intended timing and player feedback.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-objectives", "editor-testing", "editor-lighting"]),
        new("editor-objectives", "editor", "Editor: missions and objectives", "Mission setup, mode settings and objective completion checks.", "editor mission objective story terminal disk bomb modes settings win",
        [
            new("mission", "Choose a mode and mission", "<p>The map's supported modes come from its settings and authored missions. Start with one mode. Give its mission the corresponding game mode and connect the objective actors that belong to it.</p><p>Story can use terminal interactions, a carried disk with its drop zone, and bomb targets. Disk Hunt needs its own disk setup. Additional Extraction, Sabotage or Neutralization modes require missions and settings authored for those modes.</p>"),
            new("rules", "Set the playable rules", "<p>Set sensible lives, time limit and respawn values for the mode. Check objective targets and any dependency/zone progression. Player-count rules can change layout and mission win targets, so inspect each supported match size.</p><p>Make sure both teams have valid starts and can reach the relevant areas. Write player-facing objective text that says what to do and where, rather than exposing the internal actor or event name.</p>"),
            new("complete", "Finish a full round", "<p>Use <strong>Map's Own Rules</strong> in Play Options to verify the real win condition. Play Level rules are useful for exploration but remove constraints that can hide a broken match setup.</p><p>Complete every objective path, test defender interactions and confirm the result screen. Restart and repeat: one successful trigger is not enough to prove the mission resets correctly.</p>"),
        ], ["modes-and-objectives", "editor-actors-and-events", "editor-testing", "editor-map-identity"]),
        new("editor-lighting", "editor", "Editor: lighting and stealth", "Light previews, stealth analysis, baking and in-game checks.", "editor light lighting bake shadow lightmap stealth dark night vision emissive",
        [
            new("read", "Light placement and previews", "<p>Begin with readable routes and intentional dark cover. Use the editor's textured and lit views for different checks; lightmap density helps identify inconsistent baked detail. Check source-light placement before running a full bake.</p><p>A bright texture, a light's influence and how a spy reads against the background are different things. Look at the player route from the defender's height as well as from an overview.</p>"),
            new("analyze", "Use stealth analysis", "<p>The stealth map can analyze lights at start, blackout and all-lit states, or after a selected switch. Standing/crouched and night-vision views help compare conditions. These analyses operate on a compiled map and support your judgment; they do not replace a real player's view.</p><p>Wire switchable lights into the intended event chain, then check both their initial and changed states. A light that looks right in the editor may still have the wrong activation behavior.</p>"),
            new("bake", "Bake and test", "<p>Lighting settings include quality/faithful modes and adjustable density and sampling. Use fast builds during layout work and a fuller bake once the geometry settles. Imported baked lighting may need a deliberate relight rather than an accidental overwrite.</p><p>Build and play as both sides with normal vision and the relevant goggles. Test the worst sight lines after a switch and during combat. Compare the same position and facing when assessing a lighting change.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-actors-and-events", "editor-testing", "editor-import-export"]),
        new("editor-testing", "editor", "Editor: check, build and playtest", "Map Check, compiled output, play options and test coverage.", "editor map check compile validation playtest reach nav route headless build errors",
        [
            new("check", "Run Map Check", "<p>Use <strong>Build → Check Map</strong> for structural findings. The Map Check panel groups findings by severity and supports finding the affected objects. Fix errors, inspect warnings and rerun after the change.</p><p>Deep Map Check compiles the map and adds checks such as leaks, actors in the void, floating meshes, collision limits and reachability. Available fixes can help, but inspect the resulting edit rather than applying unrelated changes blindly.</p>"),
            new("play", "Test the compiled result", "<p><strong>Build and Play</strong> compiles the current map and launches OpenSCCT. Play Options controls side, mode, bots and rules. Play From Camera/From Cursor makes a local spot check easier; later lives still use the map's starts.</p><p>Use normal starts and <strong>Map's Own Rules</strong> for the final loop. Check spawn safety, traversal, doors, device placement, objectives and the round result from both sides.</p>"),
            new("matrix", "Test more than one setup", "<p>Try each supported player count and mode, then a host and joining client. A headless test round can report load and simulation failures; route, reach and navigation analysis can reveal gaps. These checks do not verify visual clarity, sound or human teamwork.</p><p>Keep a short playtest record: map build, mode, team sizes, paths attempted and failures. Retest the exact failure after fixing it, then complete another full round before sharing the build.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-objectives", "editor-blockout", "editor-import-export", "troubleshooting"]),
        new("editor-import-export", "editor", "Editor: import, export and sharing", "Import formats, runtime output and source-project preservation.", "editor import export svmap osmap t3d sdc original compile sharing",
        [
            new("import", "Import into a new project", "<p>The editor supports SVMap folders, T3D and original .sdc maps. Save an imported map as its own .osmap project and read the import report for recovered, missing or unsupported data.</p><p>Compiled maps cannot always recover all editable source information. Cooked .sdc files may have lost static-light actors; light recovery can use matching editor copies or other reconstruction paths. Inspect the recovery report and verify the result instead of assuming it is identical.</p>"),
            new("output", "Choose the target", "<p>Build produces the SVMap folder that OpenSCCT loads. The compiler reads it back through OpenSCCT's strict loader. T3D and original-format exports serve a different workflow; they need target-specific checks and, where appropriate, the original/RE+ editor.</p><p>The editor can write editor and cooked .sdc layouts, and an original-editor export folder can include supporting assets and instructions. Run <strong>Check for Original Export</strong> before relying on compatibility with the original engine.</p>"),
            new("share", "Share a tested build", "<p>Keep the editable .osmap project and its required local assets together. Share the compiled output when players need a runtime map, and keep its map ID/version clear. Opening the project is different from installing a public content pack.</p><p>Test the output with a compatible game and a separate client before distribution. Include the supported modes/player counts and known issues. Only distribute assets you have permission to share; do not assume an import makes original game assets suitable for redistribution.</p>"),
        ], ["editor-getting-started", "editor-testing", "editor-map-identity", "maps"]),
        new("editor-map-identity", "editor", "Editor: map identity and player counts", "Display names, preview images and player-count rules.", "editor identity title description dossier thumbnail picture author players count outfit conveyor",
        [
            new("identity", "Describe the map", "<p>The map identity stores the player-facing name, dossier, authors, recommended players, tags, places, translations and picture recipes. Keep the runtime map ID stable and distinct from the display title.</p><p>Write a short description of the layout and objective style. A useful title and preview help players choose the intended map variant without guessing from an internal filename.</p>"),
            new("pictures", "Preview images", "<p>Identity pictures can use supplied image files or saved map camera recipes. Mapshot renders camera pictures through OpenSCCT and caches them in the project. Rebuild or regenerate them when a changed layout makes the old picture misleading.</p><p>Check the framing at a small browser thumbnail size. Keep important areas away from the edges and use a representative playable view rather than an unfinished editor angle.</p>"),
            new("players", "Define match-size behavior", "<p>Player-count rules can control which actors are present, per-team layouts and mission win targets. Use the Match workbench to preview the intended player count, then test that count in OpenSCCT.</p><p>A recommended player number is information for players; activation rules change the actual layout. Test each supported size for starts, routes and objective completion. Map outfits and conveyors also require a compatible SVMap/runtime version.</p>"),
